# MethylSeq Pipeline: Production-Ready WGBS Analysis Workflow

[![Snakemake](https://img.shields.io/badge/snakemake-≥7.32-blue.svg)](https://snakemake.github.io)
[![Bismark](https://img.shields.io/badge/Bismark-0.24.0-green.svg)](https://www.bioinformatics.babraham.ac.uk/projects/bismark/)
[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-yellow.svg)](https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT)
[![Python 3.9](https://img.shields.io/badge/python-3.9-blue.svg)](https://www.python.org/)

A **reproducible, scalable, and production-ready** Snakemake pipeline for whole-genome bisulfite sequencing (WGBS) and reduced-representation bisulfite sequencing (RRBS) data analysis. From raw FASTQ to differentially methylated regions (DMRs) with full quality control.

## 🎯 Why This Pipeline?
Built with Snakemake, it offers:

| Feature | Benefit |
|---------|---------|
| **Automatic job recovery** | Resume failed analyses from last successful step |
| **Parallel execution** | Scale from laptop to HPC cluster without code changes |
| **Conda environment** | One-command setup, no dependency hell |
| **Modular design** | Easily add new tools or modify existing steps |
| **Built-in QC** | MultiQC reports at every stage |


## 🚀 Quick Start

### Prerequisites
- Linux/macOS (Windows via WSL2)
- Miniconda or Anaconda
- 8+ GB RAM (for human WGBS; adjust based on genome size)

### Step 1: Clone and setup
```bash
git clone https://github.com/yourusername/methylseq-pipeline.git
cd methylseq-pipeline

# Create conda environment
conda env create -f environment.yaml
conda activate methylseq
```

### Step 2: Prepare reference genome
```bash
# Download human hg19 (or your genome of choice)
mkdir -p resources/genome
wget -P resources/genome/ https://hgdownload.soe.ucsc.edu/goldenPath/hg19/bigZips/hg19.fa.gz
gunzip resources/genome/hg19.fa.gz

# Build Bismark index (required once)
snakemake --cores 8 bismark_index
```

### Step 3: Organize data
data/raw_fastq/
├── sample1_R1.fastq.gz
├── sample1_R2.fastq.gz
├── sample2_R1.fastq.gz
└── sample2_R2.fastq.gz

Update data/samples.csv with your experimental design:
sample_name,condition,fastq_R1,fastq_R2
sample1,control,data/raw_fastq/sample1_R1.fastq.gz,data/raw_fastq/sample1_R2.fastq.gz
sample2,treatment,data/raw_fastq/sample2_R1.fastq.gz,data/raw_fastq/sample2_R2.fastq.gz

### Step 4: Run the pipeline
```bash
# Dry-run to verify everything is configured correctly
snakemake -n --cores 1

# Run with 8 cores (adjust to your system)
snakemake --cores 8 --use-conda

# For HPC cluster (SLURM example)
snakemake --profile slurm --jobs 20

# Final results
snakemake --report report.zip
```